# Build-only dependency roots for the standalone Windows artifact (FR-019, FR-041).
#
# Compiled into `requirements.lock` (fully pinned, hashed) by
# `python build\build.py --lock`. `pyproject.toml` is NOT touched: its
# `pyflexicon>=4.4.1` / `PyQt6>=6.4` floors stay floors, so a FlexTools install
# is unconstrained by anything this feature pins.
#
# Four roots, per contracts/build-and-release.md section 3:

# The LCM access layer. Same distribution the module already depends on; the
# floor lives in pyproject.toml, the pin lives here.
pyflexicon>=4.4.1

# The UI toolkit. GPL-or-commercial, which is why the binary licence
# statement (FR-052) is stricter than the project's MIT source licence.
#
# CEILING, deliberately. Measured 2026-08-17: PyQt6 6.11.0 raises
#   ImportError: DLL load failed while importing QtCore:
#   The specified procedure could not be found.
# on this Windows 11 / Python 3.12.7 machine -- and it does so **unfrozen**,
# in a clean venv, so it is not a packaging fault. It fails with the matched
# PyQt6-Qt6 6.11.0 as well as with the 6.11.1 that PyQt6's own too-loose pin
# (`PyQt6-Qt6 >=6.11.0, <6.12.0`) resolves to, which rules out the version
# mismatch as the cause; Qt 6.11 appears to need a platform component this
# machine does not have.
#
# 6.7.1 is what every unit test, every live parity run and every piece of
# recorded evidence for this feature used. A build lock exists precisely so
# the shipped artifact is built from the versions that were actually
# validated, so that is what it pins.
#
# `pyproject.toml` keeps its `PyQt6>=6.4` floor untouched (FR-019/FR-041):
# this ceiling constrains the ARTIFACT, not FlexTools installs.
#
# Before raising it: install the candidate in a clean venv and check that
# `from PyQt6 import QtCore` succeeds and a QApplication constructs. The
# smoke test catches this (it did), but catching it at the lock is cheaper.
PyQt6>=6.4,<6.8
PyQt6-Qt6>=6.4,<6.8

# `gramtrans.py` opens with `from flextoolslib import *` for the six FTM_*
# metadata names, so the real library ships. A stand-in shim exposing just
# those names was considered and rejected in the spec's assumptions: it would
# be a fork of host metadata that can drift.
#
# NOTE: this drags in stock `flexlibs` (flexlibs1) and `cdfutils`
# transitively. They are inert at runtime -- nothing imports them; the module
# imports flexicon explicitly -- but they ARE components of the shipped
# artifact, and therefore fall under the FR-053 amendment's "components it
# bundles" clause and the FR-052 licence statement.
flextoolslib

# The freezer itself.
pyinstaller
